<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Huang Chao</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 884</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Huang Chao was a failed examination candidate and salt smuggler who led the largest rebellion of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>. Rising in 875, his army marched through much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a> in 879 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> in 881, where he proclaimed the state of Qi. Driven out in 883 by Tang forces and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatuo">Shatuo Turks</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-keyong">Li Keyong</a>, he was killed in 884. His former officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-wen">Zhu Wen</a> later founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/later-liang">Later Liang</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huang-chao.jpg" alt="Huang Chao" width="320" height="521" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E9%BB%83%E5%B7%A2%EF%BC%88%E6%AE%98%E5%94%90%E4%BA%94%E4%BB%A3%E5%8F%B2%E6%BC%94%E7%BE%A9%E5%82%B3%EF%BC%89.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
